<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> clean the base, melt hard wax onto it with an iron (260°F or the "wool" setting if the wax doesn't say), spread it in one smooth pass, let it cool 20 to 30 minutes, scrape it thin with a plastic scraper, then brush tip to tail. Re-wax every 3 to 5 days of riding. Rub-on wax is the quick fix in between.</p></div>

<h2>What you need</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Hard wax (an all-temperature wax is easiest): <%= gear_link "snowboard wax", "snowboard wax all temperature" %></li>
  <li>An iron (a wax iron is best; Burton says an old clothing iron works in a pinch): <%= gear_link "wax iron", "ski snowboard wax iron" %></li>
  <li>A plastic scraper, never metal, and a brush or two (nylon, then finer nylon or horsehair): <%= gear_link "waxing kit", "snowboard waxing kit" %></li>
  <li>A clean cloth, base cleaner if the base is dirty, and a stable bench or vises</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to hot wax a snowboard, step by step</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Set up.</strong> Put the board base-up on a bench or vises. Do any edge tuning or base repairs first.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean the base.</strong> Wipe it with a clean cloth. If it's dirty, use base cleaner (Burton suggests citrus-based) and let it dry.</li>
  <li><strong>Heat the iron.</strong> Use the temperature on the wax packaging. If there isn't one, evo says 260°F or the "wool" setting. If the wax smokes, the iron is too hot.</li>
  <li><strong>Drip the wax.</strong> Hold the wax against the iron a few inches above the board and drip it every few inches from tip to tail. Use as little as you can.</li>
  <li><strong>Spread it.</strong> Move the iron about 2 to 3 inches per second in a smooth pass. Never park it in one spot, especially over the binding inserts. Make a second pass for any bare spots.</li>
  <li><strong>Let it cool.</strong> Wait 20 to 30 minutes until the base is cool to the touch.</li>
  <li><strong>Scrape.</strong> Scrape tip to tail with a sharp plastic scraper at about 45 degrees until only a very thin layer is left. Not scraping enough is the most common home-waxing mistake (evo).</li>
  <li><strong>Brush.</strong> Brush tip to tail, coarse brush first, then a finer one, until the base is smooth and shiny. Burton says 15 to 20 strokes is usually enough.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Rub-on wax: the quick version</h2>
<p>Rub-on wax needs only a rag and a flat surface and usually comes with an applicator. It doesn't last as long as hot wax, but it's a good fix mid-trip. Follow the directions on the package.</p>

<h2>How often to wax</h2>
<p>For most riders, every 3 to 5 days on snow (evo). Burton says tune at least after every three uses and at the start and end of the season. White, chalky patches or "hairs" on the base mean it's dry. Re-wax after a big change in snow temperature too.</p>

<h2>Safety and wax rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Wax somewhere ventilated, and turn the iron down if it smokes.</li>
  <li>Fluorinated (PFAS) waxes are banned in FIS racing since the 2023-24 season, and Minnesota has banned selling PFAS ski wax since January 1, 2025. Fluoro-free waxes are the norm now.</li>
</ul>
